"""Foundations part 2 + first interaction-design articles."""
from _lib import *

write("millers-law",
 "Miller studied how much people hold in working memory **once information is gone**. A visible menu requires no memorising, so his finding says nothing about how many items it should have.",
 [("What Miller actually studied",
   "Miller's 1956 research asked people to repeat information they could **no longer see** — a string of digits, for example.\n\nA dropdown that stays open is a different task entirely: users scan, compare and go back. Labels and grouping decide whether they succeed, not the item count.",
   cmp("Working memory (Miller, 1956)", ["Items are no longer visible", "Must be held in mind", "e.g. repeat a digit string"],
       "A visible menu", ["All items stay on screen", "Scan, compare, go back", "Labels and grouping decide success"]),
   q("Why doesn't 7 ± 2 apply to an open dropdown?",
     ["Nothing has to be remembered — the items are visible", "Dropdowns scroll, so length doesn't matter", "Miller's sample was too small to generalise", "Menus are scanned faster than digits are recalled"],
     "Recall and recognition are different tasks.")),
  ("Two ways it gets weaponised",
   "**As a shortcut.** Nine items become seven, and nobody asks whether the labels are clear or the grouping matches users' mental models.\n\n**As a conversation-ender.** Once a famous study is invoked, questioning the decision sounds like questioning science — so nobody shows that the original was confusing or the new version is better.", None,
   q("A team cuts a nine-item menu to seven and closes the discussion. What went unexamined?",
     ["Whether the labels and grouping work", "Whether seven is the correct limit", "Whether the menu should be a sidebar", "Whether the removed items were popular"],
     "Trimming the count doesn't address the structure, which is what actually decides success.")),
  ("Don't swap 7 for 4",
   "Newer research puts working memory capacity lower than Miller's figure. The answer isn't to cap menus at four instead.\n\n**Real working-memory problems do exist** — a verification code carried between screens, values compared across pages — but those are design flaws to remove, not a rule about list length.", None,
   q("Which of these genuinely taxes working memory?",
     ["A code you must carry to the next screen", "A visible list of twelve navigation links", "A long settings page with section headings", "A table of values with a sticky header row"],
     "The information disappears and must be held in mind."))],
 [q("What does Miller's 7 ± 2 describe?",
    ["Working memory for information you can't see", "The ideal number of items in a menu", "How many choices a person will compare", "The number of steps users tolerate in a flow"],
    "It is a memory finding, not an interface rule."),
  q("What decides whether users succeed with a visible menu?",
    ["Labels and grouping", "The number of items", "The order the items were added", "How many levels deep it goes"],
    "Scanning is a recognition task; clarity matters more than count."),
  q("Why is citing a famous study as a conversation-ender a problem?",
    ["Disagreeing starts to sound like questioning science", "Famous studies are usually poorly replicated", "It takes too long to read the original paper", "Stakeholders dislike academic references"],
    "So nobody shows that the original was confusing or the new version is better."),
  q("Newer research suggests working memory is smaller than Miller said. What follows for menus?",
    ["Nothing — the finding still isn't about menus", "Menus should be capped at four items", "Menus should be replaced with search", "Items should be shown four at a time"],
    "Swapping one wrong number for another misses the point."),
  q("A verification code must be carried from one screen to the next. What should you do?",
    ["Remove the memory demand from the design", "Limit the next screen to seven fields", "Add a reminder tooltip on the second screen", "Accept it — codes are inherently hard to handle"],
    "Genuine working-memory demands are design flaws to remove.")],
 summary="7 ± 2 is about memory for what disappeared, not about how many items a menu may have.")

write("harmful-ideas",
 "Across three articles in three years, Calabro returns to the same list: useful ideas that get misused as shortcuts. Each has a real meaning and a rule of thumb that keeps it honest.",
 [("Four ideas and their alibi versions",
   "**Aesthetic-usability effect** — attractive interfaces are *perceived* as easier (Kurosu & Kashimura). The original research noted the effect is modest and varies across cultures. It doesn't reduce load, clarify state or prevent errors.\n\n**User delight** — Bill Scott's “interesting moments”: a by-product of smooth, low-effort flow that users barely notice mid-task, not decoration.\n\n**Jakob's Law** — real, but used as retroactive justification.\n\n**Element animation** — motion mistaken for polish.",
   cmp("Used as an alibi", ["Pretty = usable", "Delight = confetti", "“Google does it” = familiar", "Motion = polish"],
       "Rule of thumb", ["Task-test before polishing", "Remove effort first", "5+ similar products, similar users", "Only on user action, to show state"]),
   q("A team cites the aesthetic-usability effect to ship an untested redesign. What does the effect actually say?",
     ["Attractive interfaces are perceived as easier", "Attractive interfaces cause fewer user errors", "Visual quality predicts task completion rates", "Users forgive problems in products they find beautiful"],
     "Perception is not performance: it doesn't reduce load, clarify state or prevent errors.")),
  ("Nudging, gamification, synthetic users",
   "**Nudging** — fine when it helps the user; the test is “would the user choose this if they understood it?” Pushing the business goal is not a nudge.\n\n**Gamification** — points and streaks layered onto work that is still hard.\n\n**Synthetic users** — plausible text instead of behaviour.\n\nNone of these ideas is bad. Their **shortcut versions** are how teams ship friction with a straight face.", None,
   q("What is Calabro's test for whether a nudge is honest?",
     ["Would the user choose this if they understood it?", "Does it measurably increase conversion?", "Is it reversible within the same session?", "Has it been approved by the legal team?"],
     "If the answer is no, it is pushing the business goal, not nudging.")),
  ("The rule of thumb for animation",
   "Element-level animation earns its place only when it is triggered **by the user** and communicates a **state change**.\n\nThat is the same test as the full article on motion: if the user didn't trigger it, it probably shouldn't move.", None,
   q("Which animation passes the rule of thumb?",
     ["A row collapsing when the user clicks it", "A hero image panning on page load", "A pulsing badge on a new feature", "An auto-advancing tutorial overlay"],
     "User-triggered, and it shows a state change."))],
 [q("What do all the ideas on Calabro's list have in common?",
    ["Each is real but gets used as a shortcut", "Each was disproved by later research", "Each comes from marketing rather than UX", "Each applies only to consumer products"],
    "“None of these ideas is bad. Their shortcut versions are how teams ship friction with a straight face.”"),
  q("Whose concept of “interesting moments” does Calabro use for delight?",
    ["Bill Scott's", "Don Norman's", "Jakob Nielsen's", "Kurosu and Kashimura's"],
    "Delight is a by-product of low-effort flow."),
  q("What did the original aesthetic-usability research note about the effect?",
    ["It is modest and varies across cultures", "It disappears in expert users", "It is strongest in mobile interfaces", "It only applies to first impressions of a brand"],
    "Which is rather less than the way it gets quoted."),
  q("How is Jakob's Law misused, according to this list?",
    ["As retroactive justification for a decision", "To argue against all novel interactions", "To justify longer onboarding tours", "To defend inconsistent navigation labels"],
    "Often by copying patterns from companies big enough to train users into them."),
  q("What is wrong with gamifying a difficult workflow?",
    ["The work is still hard underneath the points", "Users find game mechanics childish", "Streaks are hard to implement accurately", "It requires too much engineering effort"],
    "Decoration doesn't remove effort — the rule of thumb is to remove effort first.")],
 summary="Seven good ideas and the shortcut versions teams use to ship friction with a straight face.")

write("stop-doing",
 "A one-page index to Calabro's recurring arguments — five habits that keep producing bad UX.",
 [("Two kinds of weaponising",
   "**Jakob's Law.** One popular app isn't a convention. Compare across a set of similar products before claiming users already know a pattern.\n\n**“Conversational UX”.** It's about how an interaction unfolds, not casual wording — and casual wording can disguise interruptions, shaming or upsells. Test whether the language helps the task.", None,
   q("A microcopy rewrite makes an upsell sound friendly. Is that conversational UX?",
     ["No — it's casual wording over an interruption", "Yes, if the tone matches the brand voice", "Yes, if users rate the copy positively", "Only if the upsell can be dismissed easily"],
     "Conversational UX is about how the interaction unfolds, not how chatty it sounds.")),
  ("Two kinds of throwing away evidence",
   "**Time-on-task data.** Don't discard it because the sample is small or the numbers look messy. Lead with the median, report the range and individual cases, and explain the slow ones.\n\n**Institutional knowledge used to shut down work.** Best practice is the default; whoever wants the exception carries the burden of proof.",
   cmp("Habit to drop", ["“Our users are different”", "Deleting the slow outlier", "One screenshot = a convention"],
       "What to do instead", ["Make the claimant bring evidence", "Median, range, and the slow cases", "Five comparable products"]),
   q("Someone says “our users are different” to stop a usability recommendation. Who carries the burden of proof?",
     ["The person claiming the exception", "The researcher making the recommendation", "Whoever has worked here longest", "The product manager who owns the area"],
     "Best practice is the default position.")),
  ("Style guides that only describe looks",
   "Document **states and behaviour**, not just colour and spacing.\n\nOtherwise developers end up making the UX decisions — at build time, without the context.", None,
   q("A style guide specifies a button's colour and padding but not its disabled or loading behaviour. What happens?",
     ["Developers decide the behaviour at build time", "The button renders inconsistently across browsers", "Designers have to review every pull request", "The component fails accessibility testing automatically"],
     "Undocumented behaviour becomes someone else's decision."))],
 [q("What does this article do?",
    ["Indexes five arguments Calabro makes elsewhere", "Introduces five new research methods", "Lists the five most common usability errors", "Ranks UX practices by business impact"],
    "It is a one-page summary pointing at the longer pieces."),
  q("What should you lead with when reporting time on task?",
    ["The median", "The mean", "The fastest completion", "The number of participants"],
    "Then the range, the individual cases, and an explanation of the slow ones."),
  q("Why is one popular app not enough to claim a convention?",
    ["A convention needs prevalence across similar products", "Popular apps change their patterns too often", "Users rarely use more than one app in a category", "The app may be under a different design licence"],
    "Compare across a set of similar products."),
  q("Which habit is on the list?",
    ["Using institutional knowledge to end a discussion", "Running too many usability tests per quarter", "Writing research plans that are too detailed", "Reporting confidence intervals to stakeholders"],
    "The other three are things he recommends doing."),
  q("What is the risk of a looks-only style guide?",
    ["UX decisions get made in code", "Designers copy components inconsistently", "Brand colours drift between products", "New hires take longer to onboard"],
    "States and behaviour belong in the guide.")],
 summary="Five habits to drop: misused laws, chatty “conversational UX”, deleted timings, “our users are different”, looks-only style guides.")

write("analogies",
 "The fastest way to explain a UX concept is to borrow an everyday object, in Don Norman's tradition. Each one is instantly relatable — which is exactly why it persuades.",
 [("Doors, ramps and spice racks",
   "**Push-or-pull door** — affordance is what's possible; the signifier is the cue that tells you which.\n\n**Curb ramp** — built for wheelchairs, used by everyone with a pram, a suitcase or a delivery trolley. Accessibility helps all.\n\n**Unlabeled spice rack** — identical jars look tidy, but you have to remember which is which. Label your controls.",
   cmp("Analogy", ["Push or pull door", "Curb ramp", "Unlabeled spice rack"],
       "Concept", ["Affordance vs. signifier", "Accessibility helps everyone", "Recognition over recall"]),
   q("Which concept does the unlabeled spice rack explain?",
     ["Recognition over recall", "Progressive disclosure", "Affordance versus signifier", "Consistency across an interface"],
     "Identical jars look tidy and force you to remember — like icon-only controls.")),
  ("The missing screw and the express lane",
   "**IKEA missing screw** — you discover it halfway through assembly. Information belongs at the moment of need, not in a manual up front.\n\nThat's the form telling you a field was required *after* you filled in fifteen of them.\n\n**Express lane** — “10 items or fewer”, with nobody enforcing it. Rules need mechanisms. If a field is required, the system should require it.", None,
   q("A form reveals a required field only after submission fails. Which analogy fits?",
     ["The missing IKEA screw", "The curb ramp", "The express lane", "The push-or-pull door"],
     "Just-in-time information: tell people before they need it, not after.")),
  ("Triangulation, in metaphors",
   "His 2023 set for explaining why one data source isn't enough:\n\n• A winemaker who samples only a few barrels → small samples\n• Reading only the reviews you agree with → confirmation bias\n• A picture with missing puzzle pieces → incomplete data\n\nCombine qualitative, quantitative, evaluative and generative work so each covers the others' blind spots.", None,
   q("Which metaphor stands for confirmation bias?",
     ["Reading only the reviews you agree with", "Sampling a few barrels of wine", "A puzzle with missing pieces", "A door with no handle on either side"],
     "You see only the evidence that fits what you already think."))],
 [q("What does the curb-ramp analogy illustrate?",
    ["Accessibility work helps everyone", "Public infrastructure sets design standards", "Regulation drives better outcomes than research", "Physical design is easier than digital design"],
    "Built for wheelchair users; used by anyone with wheels."),
  q("In the door analogy, what is the signifier?",
    ["The cue telling you to push or pull", "The fact that the door can open", "The hinge mechanism itself", "The sign giving the opening hours"],
    "Affordance is what's possible; the signifier tells you how."),
  q("What does the express lane with no enforcement represent?",
    ["Rules without mechanisms to uphold them", "A queue that is optimised for speed", "Prioritising frequent users over new ones", "A shortcut that only experts discover"],
    "If a field is required, the system should require it."),
  q("Whose tradition is Calabro following with these analogies?",
    ["Don Norman's", "Jakob Nielsen's", "Edward Tufte's", "Bill Scott's"],
    "The Design of Everyday Things made the everyday object a UX teaching tool."),
  q("Why do analogies persuade so well, according to the article?",
    ["They are instantly relatable", "They remove the need for research data", "They sound more professional than jargon", "They can be drawn quickly on a whiteboard"],
    "Relatability is the mechanism — and the reason to choose them carefully.")],
 summary="Doors, curb ramps, spice racks, IKEA screws and express lanes — five objects that explain UX fast.")
